1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
 Most people don't realize that quant traders never trust a strategy the first time that it works.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:06,820
 They run it through a loop that tests each strategy over and over again until they find

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:10,560
 one that sticks. Here's how to build this out. Most people come up with an idea, backtests once,

4
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:13,920
 and then once it looks great, they go live and two weeks later they are bleeding money. That

5
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:17,280
 strategy died because it was one guess with nothing iterating on it. A quant runs a loop

6
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:21,760
 testing strategy instead. You generate a strategy, backtest it, score it against a specific metric,

7
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:25,720
 read why it failed, feed that information back in, and then you generate a better version. Then

8
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:28,980
 you test that version, score it, read the failures, and run it again. Each round keeps

9
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:32,260
 the variance that scored well and refines the ones that did not. The whole edge in quant trading

10
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:35,760
 is the iteration and most people don't iterate at all. The loop also needs a way to score each

11
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,940
 strategy or else it just wonders. The metric that quants use is called ICIR and all it measures is

12
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:43,680
 how consistently your strategy performs over time. A strategy that makes a little bit of money every

13
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:47,160
 single week completely beats out a strategy that makes a lot of money in one week and loses it all

14
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:51,020
 in the next. That consistency score is what the loop optimizes towards. And here's the second check

15
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:54,920
 that most people skip. Even a real strategy has a shelf life. If the signal decays in two days,

16
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:58,400
 you're basically just paying fees to chase noise. If it decays in 50 days, you have a real

17
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:01,980
 traceable edge. The loop rejects these short-lived signals automatically. And this is the most

18
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:05,860
 important part. A loop that optimizes that same data that was built on does not find better strategies

19
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:09,800
 faster. It just finds prettier noise faster. Every extra iteration is just another chance to

20
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:13,260
 accidentally fit the past data. The fix that quant uses is called an out of sample gate. Every

21
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:17,600
 strategy that survives the loop gets tested one final time on fresh data that is never seen. If the

22
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:21,280
 consistency data holds on fresh data, then the score is real. If it does not, it gets thrown out no

23
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:24,660
 matter how good it looked before. I created a step-by-step guide on exactly how to build out this loop

